  mentalModels: [
    {
      id: 'mm-restaurant',
      title: 'Single Waiter, Big Kitchen (the Node runtime)',
      flow: ['Order arrives (request)', 'Waiter (JS thread) takes it, hands to kitchen (libuv/OS)', 'Waiter immediately serves next table', 'Kitchen finishes → ticket on the pass (callback queue)', 'Waiter delivers when free (event loop)'],
      explanation: [
        'One waiter (the JS thread) never cooks — cooking (I/O) happens in the kitchen (OS async + thread pool). The waiter\'s job is taking orders and delivering results, which is fast.',
        'The restaurant scales to many tables because no table makes the waiter stand and wait. It collapses if one customer makes the waiter solve a puzzle (CPU-bound work) — every other table starves.',
        'Hiring more waiters = cluster (each with their own dining room/memory). Giving the waiter assistants for puzzles = worker_threads.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Without this model you misdiagnose every performance problem — blaming "too many requests" when one slow synchronous handler is starving the lone waiter.',
      interviewTip: 'Use it to answer "how does Node handle concurrency on one thread" in 30 seconds, then drop the precise terms: event loop, libuv, thread pool, epoll.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-loop-carousel',
      title: 'The Event Loop as a Multi-Stop Carousel',
      flow: ['Stop 1: timers', 'Stop 2: pending callbacks', 'Stop 3: poll — wait here for I/O', 'Stop 4: check (setImmediate)', 'Stop 5: close', 'VIP lane between every callback: nextTick → microtasks'],
      explanation: [
        'The loop is a carousel visiting fixed stops in order. It lingers at poll — that\'s where I/O completions arrive and where it can afford to sleep.',
        'nextTick and promises are not stops: they are a VIP lane checked after every single passenger (callback) — VIPs always cut in before the carousel moves anywhere.',
        'setImmediate means "next stop 4"; setTimeout(0) means "stop 1 on the next full revolution" — which is why inside I/O (stop 3), setImmediate always wins.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Without the stops model, nextTick vs setImmediate vs setTimeout ordering is memorized trivia that falls apart under follow-up questions.',
      interviewTip: 'Narrate ordering questions by physically walking the stops aloud — interviewers reward the method even before the answer.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-backpressure-faucet',
      title: 'Backpressure as Faucet and Drain',
      flow: ['Faucet (Readable) pours fast', 'Sink (buffer) holds highWaterMark', 'Drain (Writable) empties slowly', 'Sink full → close the faucet (write() === false)', "Drain catches up → 'drain' event → reopen faucet"],
      explanation: [
        'Data flows faucet → sink → drain. The sink (internal buffer) absorbs rate mismatch but has fixed capacity (highWaterMark).',
        'Backpressure is just the rule: when the sink is full, turn off the faucet; when the drain catches up, turn it back on. pipe/pipeline are plumbers who automate the valve.',
        'No valve = water (data) on the floor (process memory), and eventually the room floods (OOM). The flood is invisible in dev because dev consumers are fast.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Every "works locally, OOMs in prod with slow clients" streaming bug comes from ignoring the valve — write() return values and drain.',
      interviewTip: 'Define backpressure in one sentence — "the consumer\'s ability to slow the producer" — then immediately name the mechanics: write() false, drain, highWaterMark.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-error-philosophy',
      title: 'Operational vs Programmer Errors: Weather vs Engine Failure',
      flow: ['Error thrown', 'Weather? (network, timeout, bad input) → fly around it: retry, 4xx/5xx, log', 'Engine failure? (bug, undefined state) → land NOW: log, exit(1)', 'Ground crew (PM2/K8s) launches a fresh plane'],
      explanation: [
        'Operational errors are weather: expected, external, survivable — your code plans routes around them with retries, timeouts, and error responses.',
        'Programmer errors are engine failure: the machine itself is broken and instruments can\'t be trusted. Flying on (catching and continuing) risks worse: corrupted writes, leaked handles, zombie state.',
        'The architecture that makes "crash on bugs" safe is statelessness plus a supervisor — a fresh plane is always ready, and no passenger luggage (state) was on board.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Teams that catch-and-continue everything accumulate zombie processes with corrupted state; teams that crash on validation errors page themselves to death.',
      interviewTip: 'The phrase "crash on programmer errors, handle operational errors" plus WHY (unknown process state) is a complete senior answer to the uncaughtException question.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-stateless-cattle',
      title: 'Processes as Cattle, Not Pets',
      flow: ['State lives outside (Redis, DB, object storage)', 'Process holds only in-flight work', 'Any instance can serve any request', 'Kill/clone/restart instances freely', 'Scaling = changing a replica count'],
      explanation: [
        'A pet server is unique and lovingly maintained — losing it loses sessions, files, caches. Cattle are interchangeable: any can be replaced by an identical one with zero data loss.',
        'Everything Node-production hangs off this: cluster round-robins requests across workers (no per-process session can survive), deploys kill processes (graceful shutdown only protects in-flight work), autoscaling adds and removes instances continuously.',
        'The test: "if I kill -9 a random instance right now, what is lost?" The answer must be "at most the requests it was actively serving".',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Sticky sessions, lost uploads on redeploy, rate limits that vary by which instance you hit, and the inability to use crash-and-restart error handling.',
      interviewTip: 'Run the kill -9 thought experiment out loud in any scaling question — it systematically surfaces every piece of hidden state.',
    },
  ],

